    wasm_mem.oot  MEM. 30k ERR wasm buf lines 39-89.
                  Bounds lines 117-143. n=4096 lines
                  145-161.
    wasm_int.oot  INT. Verdict lines 18-30. Six product
                  smokes PASS lines 64-129. wasmtime
                  47.0.3 at line 43.
    wasm_hyg.oot  HYG. Count set lines 18-21. Line
                  table lines 30-48. wasm_emit_stmt.oo
                  at 247 is the closest (line 50).
    wasm_docs.oot DOCS. Verdict lines 14-24. Residual
                  quotes lines 28-59. Honesty stays [ ]
                  at lines 54-59 and 62-66.

  Drift: INT/HYG/DOCS were first pinned by the master
  phalanx (wasm_phalanx.oot:60, 64, 67) and re-pinned by
  COORD (wasm_coord.oot:50, 56-57). Same file, two roles
  named. File content is consistent; only the wave
  claim differs.

## 6. On-disk emit entry (post-phalanx state)

  ooda/oodac/wasm_emit.oo (66 lines) imports token_fmt,
  wasm_emit_fold, wasm_emit_pick_print, wasm_emit_pick_if
  (lines 14-17). Entry wasm_emit_stream at line 37; order
  at lines 48-63 is hello fold first on main, else print
  folds then if folds. wasm_emit_pick_print.oo is 72
  lines; wasm_emit_pick_if.oo is 120 lines and imports 52
  wasm_emit_if*.oo files plus the base (lines 14-66). 90
  wasm_emit*.oo files exist on disk; wasm_hyg.oot:32-47
  lists 14, so the 76-file gap is Loop-12 mass emission.

## 7. Cross-wave conflicts and drifts

  C1 Phalanx -> COORD role split. Master phalanx named
     12 audit files (wasm_phalanx.oot:39-71). COORD wave
     added HARN2 (wasm_coord.oot:49) and a Parent rebuild
     row (line 47). 13 audit files in COORD, 12 in phalanx.
     HARN2 is a Wave B addition, not a phalanx row.

  C2 COORD -> Wave D re-pin. Phalanx pinned INT (line 60),
     HYG (line 64), DOCS (line 67). COORD re-pinned them
     inside Wave B (wasm_coord.oot:50, 56-57). On disk
     each file's role is the phalanx role. No conflict
     in file content; only in which wave claims the write.

  C3 HYG count vs on-disk. wasm_hyg.oot:18-21 names 14
     emit files. 90 exist on disk. 76-file gap is Loop-12.
     HYG did not re-count (line 50-52 says HYG does not
     split, so it also did not re-count).

  C4 Wave C vs Wave B emission. Loop-12 detectors live in
     wasm_emit_ifpneqne.oo and friends. Wave B's if-fold
     review covers wasm_emit_if.oo base (wasm_hyg.oot:37,
     121 lines). Wave C extends the if-fold. Wave C
     detectors are imported by wasm_emit_pick_if.oo:32,
     45, 63, 64, 65.

  C5 Forbidden list drift. wasm_phalanx.oot:74-78 forbids
     .py .sh .md .js and llvm_emit*.oo. Loop-12 added five
     new wasm_emit_if*.oo files. None are llvm_emit*.oo.
     Forbidden list still holds. (No drift in fact.)

## 8. Why not merge

  M1 Disjoint write set. Each Wave B file has an Isolation
     section naming one audit path. Merging B and D lets
     one owner write two audit paths, breaking COORD's
     no-two-roles-share-a-write rule (wasm_coord.oot:62).

  M2 Phalanx <-> COORD split. Wave A is the master plan,
     not a review. Wave B is a coordinated review with a
     parent rebuild. Different scope.

  M3 Loop-12 <-> if-fold. Wave C is five const-print
     shapes that extend Wave B's if-fold. Wave C has its
     own hostiles (wasm_arch.oot:12) and its own smoke
     naming (wasm_qa.oot:12-24). Merge would mix a
     5-fold cycle into a 1-fold review.

  M4 Shared vs per-role. Wave D files (rt/sec/mem/int/
     hyg/docs) prove runtime and process facts. They
     would exist with or without Wave B. COORD's re-use
     of INT to gate the rebuild (wasm_coord.oot:50,
     105-128) is a wave-internal convention, not a merge
     rationale.

